# esc-exempt.manifest — the DECLARED exemption set for ship-land's EFFECT-class escalation scan.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `esc_scan()` in scripts/ship-land.sh is the blast-radius cap on the landing
# rail: a destructive-SQL or credential pattern in the landing range PARKS a class-B decision packet
# and refuses to auto-land. Measured over its whole life (~/.claude/land.log: 506 clean / 11 hit,
# 9 distinct decision packets), its precision was ZERO — not one park was an agent about to
# auto-land a destructive migration against a durable store. What it actually caught:
#
#   4 parks  the session-index retention GC (row-deletes against session_chunks and its siblings) —
#            benign maintenance of a REBUILDABLE local cache. Still absent from origin/main as of
#            2026-07-29: the rail blocked a complete, gate-green fix four separate times.
#
# NOTE ON THIS FILE'S OWN WORDING: the destructive verbs are described, never spelled as literal
# statements. This file is not itself exempt (a config file should not be able to hide from the
# scan), so a literal example inside it would park every land that touched it — the same trap that
# drove doc authors to mangle their prose, one level down.
#   3 parks  audit/research MARKDOWN describing this very defect. One author respelled the SQL as
#            prose to dodge the scan — `<!-- pattern spelled as prose so the ship esc-scan doesn't
#            match quoted SQL in docs -->` — and it matched anyway.
#   1 park   scripts/gate-classify.sh's own `C_SURFACE` pattern table plus its bats fixtures, i.e.
#            the detector matching its own test corpus (packet shipland-esc-c661813).
#   1 park   real SQL in vendored third-party code (vendor/codex-security) — see NON-ENTRIES below.
#
# An alarm that fires only on benign input carries the same zero bits as one that cannot fire, and
# it is worse than silence: it teaches the operator to rubber-stamp the one packet class that is
# supposed to stop a real destructive land. This file restores the signal by naming, per path, where
# a destructive statement provably reaches no durable store.
#
# FORMAT (frozen contract): one repo-relative path pattern per line; `#` comments and blank lines
# ignored; leading/trailing whitespace stripped. Patterns are bash `[[ … == … ]]` PATTERN matches,
# not pathname globs, so `*` DOES cross `/` — `docs/*` covers `docs/research/x.md`. A `#` cannot
# appear inside a pattern. A missing or empty file means NO exemptions (fail-closed).
#
# SCOPE — this file exempts the EFFECT class ONLY (the destructive-SQL verbs: dropping a table,
# column, database or schema; truncating one; row-deletes; an alter that drops), whose question is
# "could auto-landing this DESTROY durable data?". It has NO power over the DISCLOSURE class
# (credential material), whose question is "does this commit LEAK a secret?" — a private key is
# exactly as leaked in a markdown doc as in code, so that class scans every file listed here and is
# never exemptible by anything.
#
# TRUST ROOT — esc_scan reads this file from the landing range's BASE revision, never the working
# tree. An entry added inside a landing range is therefore INERT for the land that adds it: a change
# can never widen the exemption set and rely on the widening in one move. That is enforced by
# construction rather than by review, which is strictly stronger than parking on touch (a
# sloppily-reviewed widening cannot take effect either), and it is why introducing this file did not
# have to park its own first land — a park-on-touch rule would have made the rail's own bootstrap
# impossible. A change here is still announced loudly at land time, because a widening nobody saw is
# the thing worth avoiding; it simply takes effect from the NEXT land.

# ── rebuildable local caches: a destructive statement here reaches no durable store ──────────────
# session-index.db is a DERIVED cache, not a store of record. Its SSOT is the transcript corpus at
# ~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl. The schema is `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` (self-creating) and
# session-index-sweep.sh walks the SSOT and re-indexes every transcript, gated only by
# file_tracking's mtime/size pair — so deleting the entire .db costs one sweep, not one byte of
# data. The retention GC this entry exists for touches exactly the derived tables (sessions,
# session_chunks, sessions_fts, chunks_fts, file_tracking) and deliberately NOT `synonyms` or
# `search_log`, which are config and telemetry rather than reconstructible index rows.
hooks/session-index-*.sh
hooks/lib/session-index-*.sh

# ── non-executing prose: a statement in a doc has no effect to cap ───────────────────────────────
# Markdown cannot execute SQL, so the EFFECT class has nothing to say about it. Three of nine parks
# were docs, and the cost was not just a blocked land: authors began mangling their own prose to
# evade the scan, which corrupts the audit record this repo runs on. The DISCLOSURE class still
# covers every file below, so a key pasted into a doc is still caught.
docs/*
*.md
# Diagram sources and their rendered output are the same non-executing prose one format down, and
# they are now the FOURTH instance of the "docs describing the defect" park class above: a flowchart
# node labelling what the landing rail guards against necessarily spells the verbs it guards
# against, and the label is copied verbatim into the generated .svg. A mermaid source cannot reach a
# database and neither can an XML drawing of one. Without this, documenting the guard parks the
# land — and the pressure is again on the author to mangle the picture until the scan stops
# noticing, which is precisely how the audit record rots.
assets/diagrams/*

# ── self-reference: pattern tables and the corpora that test them ────────────────────────────────
# An escalation classifier's pattern table necessarily CONTAINS the patterns it classifies, and its
# suite necessarily contains fixture inputs that must classify AS destructive. Neither is an
# instruction to a database.
scripts/gate-classify.sh
# tests/* is sound because hermeticity is ENFORCED, not merely conventional: the
# tests/test-hermeticity-lint.bats ratchet fails any suite that does not fixture $HOME under
# BATS_TEST_TMPDIR, so a destructive statement in a test reaches a temp fixture and cannot reach
# the operator's live ~/.claude state at all.
tests/*

# ── NON-ENTRIES (named deliberately, so the omission is a decision and not an oversight) ─────────
# vendor/*  — NOT exempt. Vendored third-party code (vendor/codex-security) executes real SQL
#             against stores whose durability this repo does not define. It is the one park class
#             in the measured population with a defensible claim to review, so it keeps parking.
# hooks/*   — NOT exempt as a whole. Only the session-index-* files above are cache maintenance;
#             the rest of hooks/ is live actuation and a destructive statement there is exactly
#             what this rail should stop.
